- Can't Stop: Four-Dice Probabilities, Strategy, and Balance (source: 03_cant_stop_addicted_to_shindig/source.txt)
    Summary: Can't Stop's four-dice probabilities and forced-move rule make it nearly balanced, and simulations show probabilistic, consistent play wins, though going first is a large advantage. (¶1, ¶2, ¶95, ¶96, ¶98, ¶100)
        - 1 Can't Stop has players roll four dice, pair them into sums, and advance markers on columns 2 through 12, needing three completed columns to win. (¶3, ¶4, ¶5)
            - 1.1 You can keep rolling as long as you want, but busting when no marker can move loses the turn's gains. (¶3)
            - 1.2 Column lengths run 3, 5, 7, 9, 11, 13, 11, 9, 7, 5, and 3 steps for columns 2 through 12. (¶4)
            - 1.3 The forced-move rule requires taking all legal moves from a chosen pairing, even unwanted ones. (¶6)
            - 1.4 Example: On {6,7,8}, rolling (1,1,3,4) and pairing 2+7 forces moving both 2 and 7 as well. (¶6)
            - 1.5 Nuance: The forced-move rule prevents trivial play and adds tactical depth. (¶6)
            - 1.6 Evidence: An interactive web version exists with a Python/FastAPI backend and React frontend. (¶5)
        - 2 Four dice flatten the distribution: P(7) rises to 64.4% and P(2) to 13.2%, far closer together than with two dice. (¶7, ¶15, ¶21)
            - 2.1 With two dice, sum 7 is most likely at 16.67% and exactly 6× more likely than sum 2 at 2.78%. (¶8, ¶9)
                - 2.1.1 Evidence: Sum 2 requires both dice showing 1 (1/36); sum 7 has 6 order-sensitive ways: (1,6), (2,5), (3,4). (¶9)
            - 2.2 Nuance: If Can't Stop used two dice, column 7 would need to be 6× longer than column 2, but it is only 13/3 ≈ 4.3×. (¶10)
            - 2.3 Example: Rolling 2, 3, 4, 5 gives just three pairings: 5 and 9, 6 and 8, or 7 and 7. (¶11)
            - 2.4 Four dice give P(can make a 2) = 171/1296 ≈ 13.2%, requiring at least two 1s among the four. (¶12, ¶13, ¶14)
                - 2.4.1 Evidence: P(zero 1s) = 625/1296 and P(exactly one 1) = 500/1296, so P(can make 2) = 1 - 625/1296 - 500/1296. (¶13, ¶14)
            - 2.5 Four dice give P(can make a 7) = 834/1296 ≈ 64.4%, via inclusion-exclusion over pairs (1,6), (2,5), (3,4). (¶15, ¶16, ¶20)
                - 2.5.1 Evidence: Each pair event has probability 302/1296, each pairwise overlap 24/1296, and the triple overlap is 0. (¶17, ¶18, ¶19)
                - 2.5.2 Evidence: Final sum: 302 + 302 + 302 - 24 - 24 - 24 + 0 = 834/1296 ≈ 64.4%. (¶20)
            - 2.6 Evidence: The full four-dice table shows at-least-one-pair counts from 171 (13.2%) for sum 2 up to 834 (64.4%) for sum 7. (¶22, ¶23)
            - 2.7 The probability ratio from column 2 to 7 is 4.88 against a length ratio of 4.33, close enough to suggest deliberate design. (¶24)
            - 2.8 The conclusion restates this: four-dice probabilities flatten the distribution, with P(7) = 64.4% versus P(2) = 13.2%, only a 4.88× difference. (¶96)
        - 3 Single columns are nearly balanced: expected rolls stay between 19.60 and 22.73 across all columns. (¶25, ¶26, ¶27)
            - 3.1 Evidence: Expected rolls equal length over probability: column 2 needs 3/0.132 = 22.73 rolls; column 7 needs 13/0.644 = 20.18. (¶26)
            - 3.2 Nuance: Column 7 is slightly faster than column 2 even though it is 4× longer. (¶26)
            - 3.3 Evidence: Expected rolls by column: 22.73 (2/12), 21.46 (3/11), 19.67 (4/10), 20.09 (5/9), 19.60 (6/8), 20.18 (7). (¶27)
            - 3.4 Evidence: Columns 2 and 12 require the most rolls; columns 6, 7, and 8 cluster near 20. (¶28)
            - 3.5 Evidence: Column 2 is only 3 steps but hit 13.2% of the time; column 7 is 13 steps but hit 64.4% of the time. (¶25)
        - 4 Three-column combinations dominate outcomes: {6,7,8} succeeds 92.0% of the time while {2,3,12} succeeds only 43.8%. (¶28, ¶29, ¶30)
            - 4.1 Evidence: {6,7,8} succeeds on 1193/1296 rolls; {2,3,12} on only 568/1296, less than half. (¶29, ¶30)
            - 4.2 Across all 165 combinations, 37 are excellent at ≥85% success, the median is 79.6%, and the worst fall to 43.8%. (¶31, ¶35)
            - 4.3 Evidence: The best combination, {6,7,8}, has 92.0% success, 8.0% bust, 39.8% clean moves, and Q = 1.43. (¶32)
            - 4.4 Evidence: The worst, {2,11,12}, has 43.8% success, 56.2% bust, 2.3% clean moves, and Q = 1.05. (¶33)
            - 4.5 Nuance: A 'clean' move hits only wanted columns; even {6,7,8} is clean only 39.8% of the time. (¶34, ¶42)
            - 4.6 Forced moves contaminate good combinations: 52.2% of {6,7,8} rolls force unwanted columns, and only 2.3% of {2,3,12} rolls are clean. (¶43, ¶44)
            - 4.7 Nuance: The forced-move rule is a balancing mechanism preventing {6,7,8} from being completely dominant, and it is the design's compensating rule. (¶44, ¶98)
            - 4.8 The conclusion restates this: column combinations matter more than individual columns, {6,7,8} at 92% success and {2,3,12} at 44%. (¶96)
        - 5 The stopping rule is EV-based: keep rolling if P(success) × Q exceeds P(bust) × U. (¶36, ¶37, ¶38)
            - 5.1 Evidence: For {6,7,8} with 5 unsaved steps: loss 0.08 × 5 = 0.4 versus gain 0.92 × 1.43 = 1.32, so keep rolling. (¶39, ¶40)
            - 5.2 Evidence: For {2,3,12} with 2 unsaved steps: loss 0.562 × 2 = 1.12 versus gain 0.438 × 1.05 = 0.46, so stop. (¶41)
            - 5.3 Nuance: Q is expected markers advanced on a successful roll; U is unsaved progress lost on a bust. (¶38)
        - 6 Simulations of 38 strategies across 14 families (3.6 million games) show probabilistic play winning and consistency beating speed. (¶47, ¶48)
            - 6.1 Evidence: Single-player, GreedyUntil1Col is fastest at 10.5 turns but has σ = 5.73 and 7.50 busts per game. (¶52, ¶53, ¶54)
            - 6.2 Evidence: FiftyPercentSurvival is second at 11.3 turns with σ = 2.86 and only 2.67 busts per game. (¶53, ¶54)
            - 6.3 Head-to-head, FiftyPercentSurvival wins 69.84% (132,696/190,000), then Heuristic(1.5) 66.46% and Heuristic(2.0) 63.26%. (¶55, ¶56, ¶57, ¶58)
            - 6.4 The champion rolls until cumulative survival probability drops below 50%, with n = log(0.5)/log(P_success). (¶59, ¶60)
            - 6.5 Evidence: It wins through sound probability math, balanced risk, speed and consistency (σ = 2.86), and only +9.42% position bias. (¶61)
            - 6.6 Evidence: Its favorite columns are middle ones: most completed 8 (42.0%), 7 (38.4%), 6 (37.7%). (¶62)
            - 6.7 Evidence: Key matchups: 55.22% vs GreedyUntil1Col, 54.38% vs Heuristic(1.5), 63.09% vs MonteCarloLookahead, 50.00% in self-play. (¶63)
            - 6.8 GreedyUntil1Col finishes in 10.5 turns but wins only 58.90%, while FiftyPercentSurvival takes 0.8 turns longer and wins 10.9% more. (¶66, ¶67, ¶68, ¶69, ¶70)
                - 6.8.1 Nuance: The fastest strategy is not the best competitive strategy. (¶66)
            - 6.9 Evidence: Core families: Greedy and Random (30% stop), Conservative(k) with k ∈ {1,2,3,4}, Heuristic(α) with α ∈ {0.3,0.5,1.0,1.5,2.0}, and OpponentAware variants. (¶49)
            - 6.10 Evidence: Other core types: Greedy-Improved, Adaptive, Proportional, progressive milestones, probabilistic, and column-count strategies. (¶50)
            - 6.11 Evidence: Newer groups 11-14 cover outside/middle preference, runner-aware, column-quality, and hybrid/advanced strategies. (¶51)
            - 6.12 Evidence: RiskBudget caps cumulative bust risk at 20% per turn; MonteCarloLookahead simulates 100 future rolls. (¶51)
            - 6.13 Evidence: The top 15 all finish in 10.5 to 12.8 turns; conservative strategies like Heuristic(2.0) average under 1 bust per game. (¶54)
            - 6.14 Evidence: A full head-to-head win-rate matrix and a downloadable CSV accompany the rankings. (¶64, ¶65)
            - 6.15 Probabilistic strategies dominate: the top 7 all use probability calculations, and pure Greedy lost essentially every game. (¶58, ¶76, ¶77)
            - 6.16 Nuance: MonteCarloLookahead, simulating 100 future rolls, only places 4th at 63.09%, so simpler rules are near-optimal. (¶78)
            - 6.17 Concession: Opponent-aware variants all underperformed simple thresholds because they only looked at completed columns, not partial progress. (¶78)
            - 6.18 For players: use FiftyPercentSurvival, aim for columns 5-9, avoid forced activation of 2-3 and 11-12, and prioritize consistency. (¶100)
            - 6.19 Nuance: For probability nerds: near-optimal play sits near 70% win rate; breaking the 75% barrier remains an open question. (¶101)
            - 6.20 The conclusion restates this: probabilistic strategies dominate, with FiftyPercentSurvival's 69.84% win rate beating even Monte Carlo 'perfect play'. (¶97)
            - 6.21 The conclusion restates this: consistency beats speed, since finishing in 11 turns reliably outperforms averaging 10.5 with high variance. (¶97)
        - 7 Simulations revealed a significant first-player advantage: Player 1 wins 55.59%, +11.18%, larger than chess (~5%) or Go (~7%). (¶71, ¶72)
            - 7.1 Evidence: Going first likely helps via first access to middle columns 6-8, setting the pace, and initiative in column selection. (¶72)
            - 7.2 GreedyUntil1Col (+31.24%) and GreedyFraction(0.5) (+31.00%) show severe position bias; OpponentAware(0.3,1,2) is high at +18.94%. (¶73)
            - 7.3 Evidence: Fairest: OpponentAware(2,1,0.3) at -4.14% favors P2, Greedy -0.03%, Heuristic(0.3) +0.65%, Heuristic(0.5) +3.30%. (¶74)
            - 7.4 Nuance: High position bias may overrate strategies like GreedyUntil1Col in aggregate statistics. (¶75)
            - 7.5 The 'opposite' OpponentAware, conservative when behind, performs better as Player 2, suggesting catching up rewards patience. (¶75)
            - 7.6 Evidence: GreedyUntil1Col wins 73% as P1 but only 42% as P2, with σ = 5.73 and 7.5 busts per game. (¶68)
            - 7.7 The conclusion restates this: the first-player advantage is significant, Player 1 winning 55.59% (+11.18%), larger than chess or Go. (¶98)
        - 8 The designers balanced individual columns but not combinations; the forced-move rule compensates, and better boards exist. (¶79, ¶80)
            - 8.1 Evidence: Single-column deviations from 20 expected rolls are at most ±13.6% on the current board, good given integer lengths. (¶81, ¶82)
            - 8.2 In multi-column play, column 7 in {6,7,8} completes in about 2 turns while column 2 in {2,3,12} takes about 30. (¶82, ¶83)
            - 8.3 Evidence: Design constraints were simplicity (the 3-5-7-9-11-13 board), 1980 manufacturability, and emotional engagement from visual length. (¶84)
            - 8.4 The forced-move rule compensates by forcing sub-optimal columns, preventing all-{6,7,8} play, and adding tactical complexity. (¶85)
            - 8.5 Evidence: Perfect balance needs lengths as exact multiples of probability: 171 steps for columns 2/12 and 834 for column 7, about 8 hours per game. (¶86, ¶87)
            - 8.6 Evidence: The current board has maximum height 13 steps and maximum deviation ±13.6%. (¶88)
            - 8.7 Evidence: A board one step higher per column (max 14) cuts maximum deviation to ±3.09%, 4× more balanced. (¶89)
            - 8.8 Evidence: A 20-step board gives lengths [4,7,11,14,17,20,17,14,11,7,4] and maximum deviation ±2.17%, a 6× improvement. (¶90)
            - 8.9 Nuance: The designers likely kept the current board for round numbers, intentional tension, 1980 testing limits, and player psychology. (¶91)
            - 8.10 Evidence: The current lengths rise by 2 steps; a linear step=2.25 design gives ±3.09%, 3.2× better balance with +1 step. (¶92, ¶93)
            - 8.11 Evidence: An integer linear design (base=5, step=4) yields lengths 5 to 25 and ±3.32% deviation, keeping odd, manufacturable numbers. (¶94)
            - 8.12 Nuance: Linear designs can never achieve perfect balance because the probability curve is nonlinear. (¶94)
            - 8.13 The author would seriously consider the 20-step board for a modern Can't Stop 2.0. (¶92)
            - 8.14 For designers: prioritize playability over mathematical perfection, compensate imbalances with rules, and consider turn-order handicaps. (¶100)
            - 8.15 The conclusion restates this: perfect balance isn't necessary, as the game is engaging despite (or because of) favoring middle columns. (¶99)
        - 9 Continuation versus switching depends on progress: the math favors continuing, but forced-move contamination can force strategic switches. (¶45)
            - 9.1 Nuance: With columns nearly complete (e.g., 10/11 steps) continuing makes sense; with 2-3 steps, switching might be faster. (¶45)
            - 9.2 Nuance: The author has not analyzed this quantitatively; comparing 'always continue' versus 'switch when X' is left open. (¶46)
        - 10 Evidence: All analysis code and simulation data are linked: probability, column-combination, strategy, and design scripts plus results. (¶102, ¶103, ¶104, ¶105, ¶106, ¶107)
